     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\"><div>Hi Jo<span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">ão, <br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">Thank you so much! I think this will solve my problem. I have two follow-up questions: <br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">1. I can also apply this formula to the 95% CIs for the betas, right</span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">? <br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">By this I mean: <br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"></span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"></span><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">- first calculate the 95% CI of the beta logit (= 1.7x 95% CI of the beta probit)</span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">- then exponentiate it to get the CIs for the odds ratios <br></span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></span></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"></span></span>Or will this cause some problems?<br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">2. Can I apply the same formula to standardized beta coefficients? I think it shouldn&#39;t be a problem, but just to make sure.<br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">Again, thanks very much<br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\"><br></span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">Best,</span></div><div><span style=\"font-size:10.0pt;font-family:&quot;Segoe UI&quot;,sans-serif\" lang=\"EN-US\">Karolina<br></span></div></div>"
